WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.275
Setting up a site by hand takes many clicks, and a second site

00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:06.973
takes them all again.

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:10.054
A recipe does that work from a single file.

00:00:10.054 --> 00:00:14.455
This video shows what a recipe is, where recipes run, how to run

00:00:14.455 --> 00:00:16.530
one, and who is allowed to.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.411
A recipe is a file that lists steps, and Orchard Core runs them

00:00:25.411 --> 00:00:27.583
in the order they are listed.

00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:33.850
A step can enable features, set the themes, create roles, define content types, or add content.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.104
Because a recipe is a file, you can keep it, share it, and

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.692
run it on another site.

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:43.470
The same steps run each time, so sites that use the same recipe

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.660
start from the same configuration.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.019
A recipe can carry a display name, a description, and tags.

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:53.628
These are what you see when you choose a recipe, so you can

00:00:53.628 --> 00:00:55.960
tell what it does before you run it.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.997
Recipes run in several places.

00:00:58.997 --> 00:01:03.634
The Setup screen runs the recipe you choose when it creates a site.

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:09.542
Tools, Recipes runs the recipes that modules and themes provide, and Tools, Deployments

00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:12.020
imports a recipe that you supply.

00:01:16.236 --> 00:01:19.825
The Setup screen of a site that is not installed asks for a

00:01:19.825 --> 00:01:22.199
Recipe, next to the name of the site.

00:01:22.199 --> 00:01:26.726
The recipe decides which features, themes, and content the site starts with.

00:01:27.179 --> 00:01:28.730
Let's open the list.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:33.144
Each entry shows the name of a recipe and a description, for example

00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:35.649
Blank site, Blog, and Headless site.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.405
Select Blog, and the button shows your choice.

00:01:39.405 --> 00:01:44.890
When you finish the setup, Orchard Core runs that recipe while it creates the site.

00:01:49.125 --> 00:01:52.428
On a site that is installed, open Tools, Recipes.

00:01:52.428 --> 00:01:57.351
The page lists the recipes of the modules and themes available to your site.

00:01:57.351 --> 00:02:02.035
The search box narrows the list as you type, so let's search for Lucene.

00:02:02.511 --> 00:02:07.163
Recipes are listed in groups, each under a heading such as The Blog Theme.

00:02:07.163 --> 00:02:11.293
Each row shows the name of a recipe and its description, with its

00:02:11.293 --> 00:02:14.201
tags as badges, and a Run button at the end.

00:02:14.654 --> 00:02:18.102
The search matches the names of recipes and of groups.

00:02:18.102 --> 00:02:22.182
A search for blog lists recipes such as Blog - Lucene Search, but

00:02:22.182 --> 00:02:26.550
not the Blog recipe of the Setup screen, because setup recipes are not

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.044
listed on this page.

00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:36.570
On the Recipes page, let's run the Admin Dashboard Widget Sample recipe.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:41.198
Its description says that it provides a sample Admin Dashboard Widget, and the

00:02:41.198 --> 00:02:44.173
Admin Dashboard feature is enabled on this site.

00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:45.579
Select Run.

00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:49.726
Orchard Core asks you to confirm, because a recipe changes your site:

00:02:49.726 --> 00:02:52.269
Are you sure you want to run the recipe?

00:02:52.759 --> 00:02:53.727
Select Yes.

00:02:53.727 --> 00:02:58.280
The page reloads with a message that the recipe has been run successfully.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.979
Orchard Core also reloads the site once the recipe has run.

00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:05.516
Open the Dashboard, the home page of the admin.

00:03:05.516 --> 00:03:09.821
It shows a widget named Orchard Core, with links to the Orchard Core

00:03:09.821 --> 00:03:12.613
site, documentation, repository, and chat.

00:03:14.213 --> 00:03:17.104
If a step fails, the recipe stops at that step.

00:03:17.104 --> 00:03:21.086
The page shows an error that names the recipe, and the steps before

00:03:21.086 --> 00:03:23.213
the failing one have already run.

00:03:23.963 --> 00:03:26.610
A recipe runs all of its steps each time.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:31.286
Running the Admin Dashboard Widget Sample a second time adds a second widget,

00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:35.003
so run a recipe again only when you want its steps repeated.

00:03:39.225 --> 00:03:44.546
A recipe can enable features, create roles, and change settings, so the Recipes

00:03:44.546 --> 00:03:46.675
page has its own permission.

00:03:46.675 --> 00:03:50.745
Let's open Access Control, Roles, and edit the Editor role.

00:03:51.195 --> 00:03:53.531
In Search permissions, type recipes.

00:03:53.531 --> 00:03:59.092
The Recipes group holds Manage Recipes, marked Security Critical, because the permission could

00:03:59.092 --> 00:04:01.705
let a user raise their other permissions.

00:04:02.178 --> 00:04:08.139
The Editor role does not have this permission, and by default only the Administrator role does.

00:04:08.139 --> 00:04:12.329
Give it to another role with extreme care, and only to people you

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:15.398
trust to run every recipe on the Recipes page.

00:04:17.398 --> 00:04:19.003
That's how recipes work:

00:04:19.003 --> 00:04:22.747
a file of steps that sets up a site, or changes an existing one.

00:04:22.747 --> 00:04:24.138
Thanks for watching.

